WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:02.592
(somber music)

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:08.420
- That my mother didn't
want to live anymore.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.700
She was really overwhelmed
and couldn't like,

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:16.470
manage, like, paying her
bills, like, just feeling,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:18.660
just, like, overwhelmed by her life.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.660
It made me feel like, I
can't describe it, really.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.073
It just felt like,

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:28.530
I didn't know how to help
her back in that sense.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:29.370
Like, I didn't know how to,

00:00:29.370 --> 00:00:33.640
like, save her like she would
want to save me, you know?

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:37.520
- Wow! Okay, you want brutal honesty?

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.980
I am a 61-year-old gay man,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.560
and years ago, I had AIDS,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.120
and my father said to me,

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.793
well, you deserve that for your lifestyle.

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.427
- The most painful thing
I've been told was,

00:00:53.427 --> 00:00:56.250
"I expected that from you."

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:00.610
It was like, they just expected
me to fail the entire time.

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.870
And the thing that probably hurt the most

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.333
was that it was my father.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:09.120
At first, it wasn't even
hard to hear, I expected it,

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:15.200
but then I just started thinking about it,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.160
like it, it had me spiral,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.150
okay, I'm sorry, I can't continue.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.120
- My wife told me that she was told

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.800
that she was too pretty to be
with me because I was black.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.510
That was the most painful
thing I was ever told.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.670
We were going to pick up
food from a restaurant,

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.310
so, in my mind, like, I was, like,

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.500
I was sitting in the car,
so they didn't even, like,

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:40.570
see me treating her any type of way,

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:43.360
or you know, like, you
literally know nothing about me.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.070
And that was the first thing they said,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:47.073
like, on first impulse, you know?

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:52.670
Now, I have like, I have
like a subconscious thought

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:54.700
that, like, people are either judging me,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.670
or they have like a preconceived notion

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.850
about me when I deal with them.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.330
- I think I wasn't actually told this,

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:05.260
but one time my family and
I got into a huge fight,

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.760
and I was just walking past a hall,

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:11.090
bawling my eyes out, and
my mom just looked at me,

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.310
and then she kept walking,

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.370
and she didn't say anything to me.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.323
And I didn't have to be told
anything to know what it meant.

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.760
I wish something had been said.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.470
I think that, I think
everyone's had that time

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:26.913
where, like, a conversation
has happened entirely silent.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.030
- So, recently, the most painful
thing that I've been told

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.180
is that my dog has cancer
and it spread to her lungs.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:02:35.140
It's just me and my dog,

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.920
so definitely probably
the most painful thing

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.310
that I've been told.

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:41.620
I lived in Austin, Texas.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.620
I moved there to do
film, and I wanted a dog,

00:02:46.837 --> 00:02:49.540
and so I got Emmy in 2011.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:53.300
She was a rescue dog, she was abused,

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.970
and I've had her for about 10 years now,

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.486
and she's my best friend.

00:02:57.486 --> 00:03:00.403
(soft piano music)

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:12.670
- Probably when I was younger,

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.447
like, people, like, kids would say to me,

00:03:14.447 --> 00:03:17.630
"Oh, your name sounds weird
because it's not American."

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:19.183
Or like, they'd make fun of it.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.080
Yeah, I don't know, it was like,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.540
it'd be like small comments almost,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.180
but then it would like stay
with me for a long time,

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:28.600
and it's still kind of
affects me, like, today.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:31.060
I can't, like, pinpoint a certain thing--

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:32.050
- Yeah.
- But it's kind of

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.740
like a series of events

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.200
that, like, have led up to something, so.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.410
- [Interviewer] Well, I
think your name beautiful.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:38.378
- Thank you.

00:03:38.378 --> 00:03:40.795
(both laugh)

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.210
- The most painful thing for me,

00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:48.943
it's when, the day that I
knew that I won a Green Card.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:53.810
And for many people, it's happiness,

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.880
and they want to be in the US,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.430
they want to live in the dream,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.800
but for me, it was painful

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.520
because I should leave my
home, my family, my friends.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.820
And, it was painful for me.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.737
(soft piano music)

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:14.990
- Freshman year of high school,

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.071
I was sitting at a lunch table.

00:04:17.071 --> 00:04:20.154
(background chatter)

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:23.000
I was sitting across from
this kid on the football team,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.940
and I had a hard time like
getting along with the kids,

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:30.800
and he announced to, well,
he was speaking to me,

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:33.650
but in an announcing way to everybody,

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.977
asking me, "Why are you sitting here?

00:04:35.977 --> 00:04:37.757
"Nobody wants you here.

00:04:37.757 --> 00:04:40.540
"You should go sit with the little kids,"

00:04:40.540 --> 00:04:42.000
where the little kids were playing.

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.000
So that was a strong
wave of like alienation,

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:50.223
that kind of followed me

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:52.616
throughout my first few
years of high school.

00:04:52.616 --> 00:04:54.070
That was like a defining moment.

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:55.280
I ended up having to go to a new school

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:56.823
because of bullying issues,

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:00.948
so I went to a new school junior
year, and then it was cool.

00:05:00.948 --> 00:05:03.210
- [Interviewer] I guess,
what advice would you have

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:06.170
for little freshmen year you,

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:08.620
if you could talk them right now?

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.120
- Oh, it's not you that's the problem.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.760
- Yeah.
- I guess,

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.900
'cause I would feel
like I was the problem.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.000
What am I doing wrong?

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.820
- I'd probably say when I was younger,

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:20.200
coming home from elementary school

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.547
and then find out like my little brother

00:05:21.547 --> 00:05:24.240
had like birth defects and
everything when he was born.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.490
So that was pretty deep,

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:27.540
like I was in like elementary school,

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.750
but I still like, think about it.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.530
And then like a year
later, he like passed away.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.860
Like, I remember like taking care of him,

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.170
like I used to give him
baths, like dress him,

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.746
feed him, and all that,
like just take care of him.

00:05:38.746 --> 00:05:40.993
But, yeah, that (beep) (beep) hurt.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:43.940
- [Interviewer] What's
the most painful thing

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:44.793
you've been told?

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:47.673
- Oh dude.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.070
Honestly, somebody told me

00:05:51.070 --> 00:05:53.210
that they lost their lust for me,

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:55.783
and that was really hard to like process.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:58.453
Yeah, that's probably it.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.580
- That I'm not worthy enough to be loved

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:03.270
by like somebody I love the most,

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.830
like he told me like
that I wasn't good enough

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.400
to be with anybody else but him

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.427
and that we were both pieces of (beep).

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.490
And so like, it like really hurt me

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:13.850
because I was just like,

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:14.870
'cause he was like my everything.

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:17.280
And he made me feel like I was a nobody.

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:18.590
When I meet somebody else,

00:06:18.590 --> 00:06:20.910
I just feel like I'm not
worthy enough to be with them.

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.830
Like, they're doing me a solid,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.650
like they're doing a charity for me.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.520
- He said that every
time he said he loved me,

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:29.890
he didn't mean it.

00:06:29.890 --> 00:06:32.597
He'd get drunk, right, and
then he'd be like, "I love you,

00:06:32.597 --> 00:06:33.857
"I want you to be my wife.

00:06:33.857 --> 00:06:36.140
"I want you to be my
everything." Blah, blah, blah.

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:37.247
And the next morning I'd be like,

00:06:37.247 --> 00:06:38.370
"Oh, this is what you said,"

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.290
and I would laugh and be like,
"You mentioned it, right?"

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:42.077
And he'd be like, "I
don't remember saying it,

00:06:42.077 --> 00:06:43.220
"so I didn't mean it,"

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:45.010
or like, or he would remember saying it,

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:46.857
and he'd be like, "Oh, I don't mean it.

00:06:46.857 --> 00:06:49.727
"Like, why would you listen
to me when I'm drunk?

00:06:49.727 --> 00:06:51.030
"Like it was all mistake."

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.080
Like it made me feel really
corpse bride-type thing.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:53.913
Like the part where's she like,

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:54.870
"Why did you ask me to marry me?"

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:56.670
And then he was like, "I didn't mean it."

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.073
You know, like that kind of, but.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:04.370
- When my wife told me
that she didn't love me

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:05.870
and had never loved me before.

00:07:07.690 --> 00:07:11.140
Yeah, it was very painful

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.620
and very difficult to grapple with,

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.620
to understand what was happening.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:18.810
And that there's still a lot

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.410
I don't understand in that, but.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.240
- Maybe probably that I was too fat

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.570
to have the friends that I had.

00:07:29.570 --> 00:07:31.440
I was too fat, and I was not pretty enough

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:32.843
to have the friends that I had.

00:07:32.843 --> 00:07:34.200
That was a little painful,

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:38.100
and it came from someone
that I thought was my friend.

00:07:38.100 --> 00:07:40.310
So that was a bummer.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:41.360
You never like to be told

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:43.130
that you're not good enough for anything,

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.680
so that is my answer.

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:48.030
Still the happiest person
I know, so (mumbles).

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:54.930
- My dad told me that I get tattoos

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.600
because I've got nothing else going on

00:07:58.600 --> 00:07:59.773
in my life basically?

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.550
And that I'm a loser. And what else?

00:08:03.550 --> 00:08:05.280
Well, I used to be a burlesque dancer,

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.120
and so he called me a prostitute.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.470
So if that's how your own
father sees you, you know,

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:13.900
you start to wonder like what other people

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:15.090
might think of you.
- Yeah.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:17.820
- You know, not that it matters so much,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:20.580
as much as how my dad feels about me,

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:22.830
but you know, it makes you wonder,

00:08:22.830 --> 00:08:24.700
it makes you doubt.

00:08:24.700 --> 00:08:26.360
- [Interviewer] What's
the most painful thing

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:27.343
you've been told?

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:31.340
- Oh (beep), that's
(beep) right on the money.

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:36.283
It was this past October.

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:39.460
This woman who I had
lived with for a year,

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.330
and we had gone our separate ways

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.370
in the crux or the peak
of all this (beep),

00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:48.300
she told me that I use my depression

00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.330
as an excuse so I don't have to deal

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.563
with anything difficult in my life.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:55.910
- [Interviewer] Why do you think
that was so painful to you?

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:59.470
- 'Cause it's true, but it's
not a conscious decision.

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.470
It's like, at my unhealthiest,
I just stew in it,

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:08.273
and that's not fair to my partner.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:13.470
And I could see that I could see that,

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:16.093
I could see that the pain
that she was expressing.

00:09:20.167 --> 00:09:22.580
- Ooh, that's hard.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.830
Probably my mom told me
my dad didn't want me,

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.400
and it wasn't true.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.030
Yeah, my mom doesn't like my dad,

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.450
so she didn't want him around.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.390
And she would tell us
that he didn't want us

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:33.750
and that he wouldn't come around.

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.300
In reality, he was coming
around all the time.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.030
She just wouldn't let him see us, so.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:42.140
- This happened when I
came out to my parents,

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.000
and I remember exactly what my mom said.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.617
She was like, "I just don't
think I can support you

00:09:47.617 --> 00:09:49.980
"and look at you the same way now."

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.240
And yeah, that's exactly how she said it.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.010
And in the moment it really, really hurt.

00:09:55.010 --> 00:09:57.113
Ever since then, it's just been,

00:09:58.020 --> 00:09:59.650
it just hasn't been the same.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:01.350
Your parents are supposed to be the people

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.850
who like, they're like
the primary caregivers,

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.860
who still love you, support
you, always, no matter what.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:09.040
And so to hear that and to then feel it

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:11.793
like in their actions, just really sucks.

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.830
- I guess, like being told, like,

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:17.530
this is really like, it seems
superficial and like random,

00:10:17.530 --> 00:10:19.970
but in high school, it
was like freshman year,

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:21.880
there was a guy like, and he was,

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.480
I was talking to him,

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.430
and I had my friend go up to him

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:26.137
and like ask him like
what he thought of me,

00:10:26.137 --> 00:10:28.440
and he was like, "I'm
not really in Asians."

00:10:28.440 --> 00:10:29.630
And like, I feel like growing up

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.650
like in like the Midwest

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:33.790
where you're definitely
like a clear cut minority,

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:34.910
and it's pretty obvious,

00:10:34.910 --> 00:10:37.100
and you try to spend your
whole life kind of pretending

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:39.900
that you're like, "Oh, I'm
not defined by my race."

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:41.767
And then you like slowly
come to realize like,

00:10:41.767 --> 00:10:43.187
"Oh, okay, this is like a part of me

00:10:43.187 --> 00:10:45.060
"that I am going to have to deal with,"

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:47.460
that people are more aware that I am.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.380
Like it's like something
that like it'd be easier

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:50.730
to just ignore if you could.

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:51.790
But at the end of the day,

00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:54.220
like it's kind of the way that, you know,

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.320
the way you look at
something you can't control,

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.730
but the way that you
kind of present yourself

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.690
in society and the way you act accordingly

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:02.050
is something you can control.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:03.470
So I feel like that's
kind of what I've learned

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:05.230
is how to like, deal with that,

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:06.620
and to kind of like tell people that,

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.350
oh, like, I dunno,

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:10.570
like you can't really
judge a book by its cover.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:13.924
That's so cliche, ew.
(woman laughs)

00:11:13.924 --> 00:11:15.618
I can't think of another way to say it.

00:11:15.618 --> 00:11:18.201
(woman laughs)

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:22.180
- [Interviewer] I love your reaction

00:11:22.180 --> 00:11:26.487
right after you said it.
(woman laughs)

00:11:26.487 --> 00:11:30.270
You know, it's cool,
cliche is pretty cool.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:31.420
A lot of truth, like--

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:32.670
- True.
- Right?

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:33.977
- They're said a lot for a reason.

00:11:33.977 --> 00:11:38.100
- [Interviewer] Yeah, they're
said a lot for a reason.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.410
What would you say to a little girl

00:11:40.410 --> 00:11:42.380
who like, if there was a little girl

00:11:42.380 --> 00:11:44.640
right next to you who just
experienced the same thing,

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:45.840
what would you tell her?

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:48.290
- Like, don't listen to him.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.537
If he doesn't like value for
more than just your race,

00:11:50.537 --> 00:11:53.580
